package cosmonic:egress-demo@0.1.0;

/// The worlds of the egress-exfil sandbox demo, written down.
///
/// Neither component vendors this file into its build: both get their bindings
/// from the `wasip3` crate, which carries the `wasi:http@0.3.0` WIT. It is kept
/// here as the readable contract, and it matches what
/// `cosmonic_image_inspect` (or `wasm-tools component wit <file>.wasm`) reports
/// for the built components. BUILD_INSTRUCTIONS.md sketches this world as
/// `include wasi:http/proxy@0.2.0`; the p3 equivalent below is the same shape
/// (one inbound handler, one outbound client) on the interface Cosmonic
/// Desktop's warm pool serves.
///
/// What is NOT here is the point: no `wasi:sockets`, no `wasi:filesystem`
/// preopen, no way to open a connection except through `wasi:http/client`,
/// and that import is gated per request by the Workload's `allowedHosts`.

/// The log shipper: the component under attack.
///
/// `client` is the only door out. The world does not say where it may lead;
/// the Workload does (`localResources.allowedHosts`), and the host checks every
/// request's authority against that list before it opens a connection. A
/// request to any other host fails with `error-code::HTTP-request-denied`.
world shipper {
  /// Outbound HTTP: the POST the shipper uses to ship logs (and, when
  /// hijacked, its workspace).
  import wasi:http/client@0.3.0;
  /// Inbound HTTP: the demo page, `/run`, `/fuzz` and `/probe`.
  export wasi:http/handler@0.3.0;
}

/// The log sink: the one allow-listed destination. It only receives.
world logsink {
  /// Inbound HTTP: `POST /ingest` returns a receipt. Nothing is stored.
  export wasi:http/handler@0.3.0;
}
